rows.old_statement_tex:
\label{prop:section5-local-branches}
Fix $\d\ge11$. For each $1\le n\le\d$, there is a real-analytic function $r_{\d,n}(\delta)$ near $\delta=0$ with
\begin{equation}
 r_{\d,n}(\delta)=-\frac{\delta}{2a_\d+b_\d(n-1)}+O(\delta^2).
 \label{eq:section5-branch-amplitude}
\end{equation}
For $\tau>1$ sufficiently close to one, the sufficiently small nonzero critical points of $\Phi_{\d,\tau}$ are exactly
\begin{equation}\label{eq:section5-branches-explicit}
 \begin{aligned}
 u&=v_{\delta,I,x_0}+w(\tau,v_{\delta,I,x_0}),\\
 v_{\delta,I,x_0}(x)&:=2\sqrt{r_{\d,|I|}(\delta)}\sum_{j\in I}\cos\bigl(2\pi(x_j-x_{0,j})\bigr),
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
where $\varnothing\ne I\subseteq[\d]$ and $x_0\in\T^\d$. Thus $I=\{j:z_j\ne0\}$, and each $I$ determines one branch modulo translations. Writing $n=|I|$, the value of the functional on that branch is
\begin{equation}
 \Phi_{\d,\tau}^{(n)}=-\frac{\delta^2}{4q_{\d,n}}+O(\delta^3).
 \label{eq:section5-n-branch-pressure}
\end{equation}
Every $q_{\d,n}$ is negative. The branches with $n<\d$ are saddles. The branch with $n=\d$ is a Morse--Bott local maximizer modulo translations\footnote{The Morse--Bott condition means that the critical points form a smooth manifold and the kernel of the Hessian at each point is exactly the tangent space to that manifold; see \cite[Section~2.6]{Nicolaescu2011}. Here the manifold is the translation orbit, and the Hessian is strictly negative in the transverse directions.} and has the largest value of $\Phi_{\d,\tau}$ among the nonuniform local branches.

